/** @mainpage Qore yaml Module

    @tableofcontents

    @section yamlintro Introduction

    The yaml module provides <a href="http://www.yaml.org">YAML</a> functionality to Qore, allowing qore programs to
    read and write information in %YAML syntax.

    This module is released under a choice of two licenses:
    - <a href="http://www.gnu.org/licenses/old-licenses/lgpl-2.1.html">LGPL 2.1</a>
    - MIT (see COPYING.MIT in the source distribution for more information)
    .
    The module is tagged as such in the module's header (meaning it can be loaded unconditionally regardless of how
    the %Qore library was initialized).

    Independent parser and writer instances can be used concurrently. A document iterator or stream writer
    maintains stream position; callers sharing an instance must synchronize the complete sequence of operations.

    The underlying %YAML functionality is provided by <a href="http://pyyaml.org/wiki/LibYAML">libyaml</a>.

    User modules implementing the following HTTP-based protocols using YAML for data serialization are included:
    - YAML-RPC: similar to JSON-RPC but using YAML-serialized data
      (@ref yamlrpcclientintro "YamlRpcClient",
      @ref yamlrpcclientiointro "YamlRpcClientIo" (async I/O),
      @ref yamlrpchandlerintro "YamlRpcHandler")
    - DataStream: allows for data streaming with HTTP 1.1 chunked data transfers where each chunk is a unique data
      entity (@ref datastreamutilintro "DataStreamUtil",
      @ref datastreamclientintro "DataStreamClient",
      @ref datastreamclientiointro "DataStreamClientIo",
      @ref datastreamrequesthandlerintro "DataStreamRequestHandler")
    - Connect (DataStream 2.0): HTTP-native streaming RPC protocol with 5-byte framed messages,
      all 4 RPC patterns, and gRPC wire format compatibility
      (@ref connectintro "Connect")

    @section Examples

    @par To serialize a simple value or a complex data structure to a YAML string:
    @code{.py}
%modern

%requires yaml
string yaml_str = make_yaml(data, YAML::Canonical);
    @endcode

    @par To deserialize a YAML string to a Qore value:
    @code{.py}
%modern

auto data = parse_yaml(yaml_str);
    @endcode

    @section yaml_scalar_resolution Numeric Scalar Resolution

    Plain decimal numbers require at least one mantissa digit and, when present, at least one exponent digit.
    For example, \c .5, \c 5., and \c 1e3 are floats; \c ., \c -., \c +., \c .e3, and \c 1e2+3 are strings.
    The YAML special float spellings \c .inf, \c .Inf, and \c .INF accept an optional sign; \c .nan,
    \c .NaN, and \c .NAN represent NaN without a sign. Quoting these YAML spellings preserves string values;
    literal and folded block scalars also remain strings.
    The same rules apply to parse_yaml(), parse_yaml_documents(), YamlDocumentIterator, and YamlSaxParser.
    An explicit \c !!float tag requires a valid float value; invalid values raise \c YAML-PARSER-ERROR.

    Qore's existing numeric extensions, including <tt>\@inf\@</tt>, <tt>\@nan\@</tt>, and arbitrary-precision
    literals such as \c 5e+02n{128}, remain supported. Single-quoted Qore numeric extensions retain their
    existing special interpretation; use double quotes to preserve them as strings.

    @code{.py}
%modern
%requires yaml
hash<auto> data = parse_yaml("description: .\nlimit: .inf\nratio: .5\n");
@assert(data.description == ".");
@assert(data.limit.infp());
@assert(data.ratio == 0.5);
@assert(parse_yaml(make_yaml(".inf")) == ".inf");
    @endcode

    @section yaml_core_schema Documents Written by Other Tools

    By default, plain scalars are also resolved to Qore's extended types: ISO 8601 timestamps become absolute
    dates, ISO 8601 durations with at least one component become relative dates, and \c sqlnull and numeric
    suffixes have their Qore meanings.  A JSON-compatible document written by another tool, such as an OpenAPI
    description, has no such types: parse it with the @ref yaml_parse_option_constants "YAML::ParseCoreSchema"
    option, which resolves plain scalars with the YAML 1.2 core schema only, so every other value keeps its text.

    @code{.py}
%modern
%requires yaml
hash<auto> api = parse_yaml("example:\n  created_at: 2025-03-05T14:59:25Z\n  country: PT\n",
    YAML::ParseCoreSchema);
@assert(api.example.created_at == "2025-03-05T14:59:25Z");
@assert(api.example.country == "PT");
    @endcode

